WEBVTT

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.900
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.110
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.070
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:43.080
<b>Chapter Ten               Aster-Colored Truth</b>

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.140
We finally meet, Saikawa-sensei.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.970
Hello, Dr. Magata Shiki.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.450
Sensei, what are you saying?

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.170
Nishinosono-san is also there, is she not?

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.520
I'd like to see her.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.550
Same here.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.950
Shall we go to where you are now?

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.610
No, this is a special occasion,

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.710
so let us meet somewhere special.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.680
Don't stare at me so much.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.540
I'm just trying to copy your movements.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.190
Sensei...

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:22.200
I understand you want to see Dr. Magata,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.590
but wake up.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.500
We saw her body.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.350
"Who are you?"

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.600
Huh?

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.780
When you tested this device with Shimada-san, 
you were asked that, right?

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.630
I've heard that question before.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.400
Because someone is pretending to be the doctor.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.470
How do you feel?

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.550
Like I'm about to embark on 
a journey through space.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.220
Have a wonderful trip, then.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.580
They're both inside.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.100
I don't really understand,

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.890
but we can hear the killer's voice now, right?

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.250
Yes...

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.310
What about video?

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.540
Impossible.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.490
It's only visible within their minds.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.490
Chief, once we identify their access 
point, we'll head in immediately.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.850
The killer says they'll only talk to those two.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.000
Not happening. We're the police.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.170
But isn't it a bad idea to tick the killer off?

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.630
That's a vicious murderer.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.510
For now, we'll wait until we get a confession.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.310
I'll give the word.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.810
Sensei, can you see it?

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:34.750
Yeah.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.420
I can.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:38.950
The sea is beautiful.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.190
The sea?

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.300
Nishinosono-san...

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.740
We meet again, yes?

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.350
I'll start by asking a question.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.490
Did you factor in that I'd solve your problem?

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.790
Nishinosono-san,

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:02.090
did you tell Saikawa-sensei 
about seven being lonely?

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:02.870
Yes.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.480
And "B" and "D"?

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.410
I didn't tell him that.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:08.220
I didn't know what they meant.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.600
I understand.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.480
If I'd heard that, I probably would've 
figured it out an hour earlier.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.480
Such confidence.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.760
Wait.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.720
You're just pretending to be Dr. Magata, right?

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:20.940
Who are you, really?

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.190
Calm down, Nishinosono-kun.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.150
I believe you'll tell us the truth now.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.750
Very well.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.820
I shall answer any questions you have,

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.250
since the game has ended.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.390
You're the one who killed those 
two people, aren't you?

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.330
Correct.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:40.730
Where's the access point?

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.550
I don't know yet... Please calm down.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:47.180
All right, where to begin?

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.800
Wherever you like.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.110
It would have to be this, then.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.060
The biggest mystery in this case.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.190
"Everything becomes F."

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.250
"F" is "fifteen."

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:03.490
In your room, the dictionaries 
and literature collections

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.490
all stopped at volume number fifteen.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.490
I'm impressed you noticed that.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.860
It wasn't a coincidence, was it?

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:12.720
Your daughter...

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.470
Daughter?

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.370
...was always taught by you.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.420
And you had no need to teach her 
numbers higher than fifteen.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:23.470
Because humans only live for fifteen years.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.770
In their fifteenth year, they turn 
fourteen, and kill their parents.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.360
Just as you did when you were fourteen.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:30.550
No way.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.780
Sensei, this isn't Dr. Magata we're talking to!

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.660
Whom was it you met at the lab?

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.600
Dr. Magata Shiki.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.220
She didn't look twenty-nine.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:41.630
That's what you said.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:45.120
That's because she spent fifteen years in her lab.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.180
I don't think that's the case.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.720
You didn't meet Dr. Magata Shiki.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.010
It was her daughter.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:54.580
That's impossible...

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.100
Your daughter was meant to do as you told her.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:00.580
Kill you and take your identity.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.850
You'd planned that for years.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.590
You made her wear gloves all the time,

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.410
so she wouldn't leave fingerprints.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.940
The reason Magata Shiki began to publicly

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.600
appear so much lately was to make 
the world believe she was you.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.980
Nishinosono-kun, that was also 
true of when you met her.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.100
No way.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:22.620
The whole thing had been planned out.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.800
She would kill her mother and go outside.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:27.810
And then kill her father.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.080
That's what her mother taught 
her to do so many times.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.310
She never thought about if it was right or wrong.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.400
She'd always believed that was 
the only way humans lived.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.260
And then, it happened.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.450
When your daughter became F. That is, fifteen.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.090
You haven't explained everything.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:52.460
Also, Sensei, there are other F's.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.220
"Fourteen," "five," "four."

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.230
Hexadecimals, Nishinosono-kun.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.330
The variable in Red Magic

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:05.600
that keeps track of time is two-bytes long.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.360
In hexadecimal, it can hold up to four digits.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.010
Sixteen to the fourth power...

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.360
65,536.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.290
Taking into account that it starts 
at zero, it's actually one less.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:20.120
65,535.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.180
What does that have to do with F?

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:28.010
In the world of hexadecimals, 
a single digit can go up to fifteen.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:31.160
In decimals, the digit after nine,

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.110
or ten, becomes a two-digit number.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.010
In the same way,

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:37.700
in hexadecimals, the number after fifteen

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.140
becomes a two-digit number.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:45.440
Thus, numbers greater than nine should 
still be treated as a single digit.

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:50.060
So, in hexadecimals, the decimal ten is "A,"

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.340
and eleven is "B."

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.950
Then, what is "F" in hexadecimals?

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.450
Fifteen... Everything becomes fifteen.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:04.390
The maximum decimal value of 65,535

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.170
becomes, in hexadecimals, FFFF...

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:13.220
In other words, the largest number 
Red Magic can count to.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:14.760
Right!

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.260
FFFF is the maximum number it can 
count to in hexadecimals, right?

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:20.450
Yes.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.230
It counts from 0000,

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:27.010
all the way to FFFF.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.270
That was the timer.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.600
You've been planning this

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.450
for a minimum of seven years.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:39.030
Yes.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:39.990
And that day,

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.640
the counter reached its limit and 
executed its programming.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:48.000
Yes. Everything became F.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.940
You see, Saikawa-sensei,

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.850
I am a Trojan horse.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:54.460
I understand.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.600
What does she mean, Sensei?

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.890
Nishinosono-kun, what's a Trojan horse?

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.770
A program that appears useful,

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:07.340
but actually contains destructive code.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:11.590
But when it destroys things, it doesn't 
act irregularly, like a virus.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:14.900
It destroys things, like a virus, 
while acting normally...

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:16.860
That's about right.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.870
Red Magic didn't suddenly start acting up.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:24.600
A normal program that had been 
part of Red Magic from the start

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.820
began acting just like a virus would.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.420
In other words, everything was 
planned from the beginning.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:34.510
Back then, just as it was programmed to do,

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.310
Red Magic kept that yellow door from opening.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:40.770
And then, at the appointed hour,

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.610
you opened that door.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:47.520
The Trojan horse here was 
Red Magic's main system itself.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:51.730
Put another way, its designer, Dr. Magata Shiki...

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:52.940
You.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:56.890
Did you see video of me leaving the room?

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:58.190
Unfortunately, no.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:01.700
Because you set the lab clocks back by one minute.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.060
I am impressed that you noticed.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.150
Though it seemed to take you some time.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:09.310
There's one thing I'm wondering about.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.180
Why didn't you erase all traces of the Trojan horse?

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.710
Because I wanted someone to notice.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.520
Someone to notice like you,

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:20.680
Saikawa-sensei.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:23.810
Who are you?

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:25.690
That's the third time for that question, isn't it?

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:27.480
Answer the question, please.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.690
I am Magata Shiki.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:30.730
No, you are not!

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:32.590
Find the data for the access point now!

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:33.320
Wait.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:35.800
Let's listen to her a little more.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.200
How long will you pretend to be Dr. Magata?

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.630
You killed your parents.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.910
Your mother, the doctor, and your 
father, Director Shindo.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.010
The doctor and the director were her parents?

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:48.930
No way!

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.940
Dr. Magata killed her parents because they opposed

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:55.380
her giving birth to the child she 
conceived with Director Shindo.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:56.850
Yes, that's right.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.160
I could not accept it.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.460
Because no one had told me it was wrong.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:04.960
"A doll did it."

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.640
That's what you said.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.070
You meant a body.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:09.500
In other words,

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:13.850
you, the only one of the multiple 
personalities with a body.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:15.930
Who knows? I couldn't say.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:17.620
Enough!

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.030
Why did you kill Dr. Magata?!

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.510
That is something permitted to all 
living creatures, Nishinosono-san.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:26.790
Permitted?

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:29.010
What are you talking about?

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:32.370
A daughter killing her mother is a terrible thing!

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.630
Don't cry, Nishinosono-san.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:36.240
That is the destiny

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.090
under which all creatures are born.

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.860
A flower wilts for the sake of a new seed.

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.070
And a mother dies for the sake of a new life.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.100
This is not a sad thing.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.460
Shut up!

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.520
How can losing your family not be a sad thing?!

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.710
It isn't okay to kill your parents.

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:57.860
But she wanted to give birth to you, anyway.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.110
And she raised you in that room.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:03.360
That's why that room needed a robot that

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.000
could open and close doors.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.680
Because you locked the door from inside.

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.460
And there were two chairs in the room for you,

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:13.710
and blocks, and stuffed animals.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.500
No one went in or out of that room,

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:18.340
but the killer was inside.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.380
And it was only possible because you 
were in that room for fifteen years.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.310
The doctor told you to kill her.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:29.100
That was her attempt at purifying herself, 
after killing her own parents.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:31.890
That isn't true, Nishinosono-san.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:33.310
What isn't true?!

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:34.650
Nishinosono-kun,

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:37.170
I thought the same thing at first.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:38.440
But the truth is different.

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:40.090
What do you mean?

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.710
Then, I'll ask you...

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:45.530
Why did she, the doctor's 
daughter, kill her mother?

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.450
Because the doctor told her to.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:53.110
So why did she cut off her limbs?

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:55.460
The doctor never told her to do that.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:58.090
Because she needed

00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:00.230
the doctor's hands to leave the room.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.530
And her legs? Did she need those, too?

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:03.970
Well...

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.280
Your reasoning isn't logical.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.470
It's very emotional.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:12.430
The killer wasn't the doctor's daughter.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.520
It was the person right in front of us,

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:17.760
Dr. Magata Shiki.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.280
Then the body we saw?

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:23.860
The doctor's daughter.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.390
She was going to kill her parents.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:28.370
Until she met you.

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.990
Until you asked her that question.

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:34.710
"Who are you?"

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:37.770
That question threw her off.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:40.460
Because it would reveal she wasn't Magata Shiki?

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:41.800
No.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.860
From that moment, she began to question it.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:47.470
Exactly who she was.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:50.590
Your reasoning is very emotional.

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:53.290
Yes, I'll admit the contradiction.

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:54.310
That's not fair.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:57.490
That is why she couldn't have killed her parents.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.730
"That is why"? What do you mean?

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:03.390
In other words, she was a normal girl.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.210
She wasn't a genius, like the doctor.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:09.040
So she couldn't understand the doctor's reasons.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:11.240
"Who am I?"

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.460
Your question caused her to hesitate.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.870
And she wanted to be free.

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.420
That's why she wanted to

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:21.380
kill the doctor and go outside.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:24.060
That isn't the only way to be free.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:25.630
What do you mean?

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.620
For instance, I'm free right now.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.950
I've been released from my body, 
which is simply a vessel,

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.610
and am floating, without purpose.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:35.370
Freedom itself.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:39.050
I would be happy staying here forever, 
never going back to my original world.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:42.150
I don't understand what you're saying.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:45.070
Is that really true, Nishinosono-san?

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.670
There must have been a time when 
you wanted the same thing...

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:51.570
To be released from everything that bound you,

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:53.230
to be free.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.930
But you did not do that.

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:58.580
Because Saikawa-sensei was with you.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:02.420
She, herself, chose death?

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:03.610
I don't know.

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:04.590
Regardless,

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:09.310
you cut off her limbs, to make 
it appear that you had died.

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:12.700
The hands, so the fingerprints wouldn't identify her.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.900
And then the legs, so your actions 
wouldn't be obvious.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.660
And you went from the elevator to the roof,

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:24.110
waited for Director Shindo to arrive, and killed him.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.150
He turned his back on you

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:30.110
because he loved you. Isn't that right?

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:32.860
Yes, that is correct.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.970
Then afterward, where did you disappear to?

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:37.870
She's right in front of us.

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.620
She's not the doctor.

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:41.780
And this isn't reality!

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.450
In the physical sense,

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:47.300
she's accessing this system from 
a terminal somewhere.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:50.290
Isn't that right, Dr. Magata Shiki?

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:54.590
Splendid, Saikawa-sensei.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.450
But I am currently disinterested 
in the material world.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.390
I want to stay here a bit longer,

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:03.210
Saikawa-sensei.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.400
Will you come with me?

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:16.160
Sensei! Sensei!

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.370
What's going on? She disappeared?

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:21.500
Sensei! Don't leave me alone!

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:22.280
Sensei!

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:23.380
Moe-chan!

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:25.280
Where's the girl?

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:27.020
We don't know.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:28.990
She's probably using a very complicated route.

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:30.600
Search the lab!

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.550
Strengthen security at the 
entrance and on the roof.

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:33.310
Right away!

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:33.930
Okay!

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.320
Where are we?

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:40.040
Don't ask.

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:44.440
What's important is where it is to you.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:49.280
I wonder if we see the same thing.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:52.060
I don't know.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:54.520
We are free here.

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:55.380
Yes.

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:57.560
Now, what shall we do?

00:16:57.560 --> 00:16:58.860
Anything.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:01.920
There are no rules to bind us here.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.130
I want to see the ocean. It's been a while.

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:09.350
That was the message you left.

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:12.950
In that moment, what were you feeling?

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:14.970
Which moment?

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:17.470
When you came out onto the roof of the lab.

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:19.850
Nothing, really.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:21.960
Is that true?

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.080
It had been fifteen years since 
you'd seen the sky.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:33.790
You're quite sentimental, are you not?

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.550
That is your weakness.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:38.920
Perhaps.

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:43.380
Saikawa-sensei, can you keep up with me?

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:50.360
We're inside the sea, right?

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:53.780
I said something stupid again.

00:17:55.390 --> 00:17:58.970
Saikawa-sensei, what are you thinking now?

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.380
I want a cigarette.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:02.940
Why are you thinking that?

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:05.460
Because you can't smoke underwater.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:08.780
Wonderful...

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:11.240
I am glad I met you.

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:12.610
Me, too.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:16.610
Someday, I shall come to you, for certain.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:18.560
Remember that.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:21.620
Can we shake hands here?

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:27.120
Who are you?

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:46.950
Saikawa-sensei? Saikawa-sensei!

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:50.340
Sensei!

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:51.870
I'm so glad...

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:57.650
The autopsy showed no signs of past pregnancy.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:18:58.970
No way.

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:01.660
Then it really was...

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:03.790
But where did the doctor go?

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:05.560
We're searching the lab now.

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:07.940
There's a chance she went outside.

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:11.670
That's impossible, as long as 
the system here is working.

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.540
During that time, it wasn't.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:14.870
Yamane-san?

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:15.690
Yeah...

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:19.750
For some reason, it suddenly switched 
to Unix, and the lights went off.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:21.350
It wasn't "for some reason."

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:23.590
The doctor changed it.

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:26.730
But you'd need a ship to leave the island.

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:27.610
A ship...

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.170
Setsuko!

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:32.190
What is it, Sohei-kun?

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:34.000
You passed by our students

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.450
when you arrived, right?

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:37.070
Yes.

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:39.380
You said there were two women, right?

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:40.410
That's right.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:41.980
One was Chinese?

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:43.010
That's true.

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:44.200
She couldn't speak properly.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.110
And the other?

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:49.260
Um... She had a purple dress, and...

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.710
Kunieda-kun would die before she wore a dress.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:57.460
Why didn't I realize it?

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:09.310
I'm a fool.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:12.070
Sensei...

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:15.150
There never was a bag.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.230
There's no such person as Magata Miki.

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:21.020
She was Dr. Magata Shiki.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:23.830
Magata Shiki is a genius.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:25.830
It's impossible for us to match her.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.870
It was never even a contest.

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:50.240
I missed you!

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:51.570
Me, too.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.010
You're going to kill me now, right?

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:55.590
Don't cry.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.230
I want to save you.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:01.840
I wasn't going to give you this, but...

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:07.740
I'm really glad I've lived this long.

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:10.740
Farewell, Uncle.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:14.270
I loved you, Uncle.
